        This is a solitary, well-circumscribed, pigmented lesion measuring approximately 1.5 cm in maximum dimension. Morphologically, it presents as a plaque, characterized by its relatively flat to slightly elevated surface and broad, non-eroded borders. The lesion exhibits a heterogeneous, multicolored pigmentation pattern, predominantly composed of varying shades of brown, ranging from light to dark brown, with areas of darker, almost black pigmentation centrally and peripherally. The pigmentation is not uniformly distributed, showing a mottled, uneven appearance with visible pigment clumping and focal areas of increased density. The borders are irregular and somewhat jagged, with a scalloped contour, and appear to be well-defined against the surrounding normal skin, though there is a subtle, faint peripheral halo of lighter pigmentation. The surface texture is rough and verrucous, with prominent scaling and fine, superficial fissuring, suggesting chronicity and possible keratinization. There is no evidence of ulceration, crusting, or telangiectasia. The lesion is asymmetric in shape and lacks any discernible radial symmetry or internal structural patterns such as a pigment network, globules, or regression structures. The overall appearance is consistent with a benign acquired nevus, specifically a compound or intradermal nevus, exhibiting features of chronic sun exposure and surface keratinization.

