  Past Findings: This is a solitary, well-circumscribed, pigmented lesion measuring approximately 1.5 cm in maximum diameter, located on the skin surface. Morphologically, it presents as a plaque, characterized by a raised, flat-topped, and slightly elevated lesion with a broad base. The lesion exhibits a heterogeneous coloration, predominantly composed of varying shades of brown, ranging from light to dark brown, with a central area of darker, almost black pigmentation and a surrounding zone of lighter brown. The pigmentation is not uniformly distributed, showing a mottled or variegated appearance with areas of increased and decreased density. The borders are irregular and poorly defined in places, with some areas appearing to fade gradually into the surrounding skin, while other portions maintain a more distinct margin. The surface texture is relatively smooth but exhibits fine scaling and mild hyperkeratosis, particularly along the periphery. There is no evidence of ulceration, crusting, atrophy, lichenification, or telangiectasia. The lesion demonstrates asymmetry in its pigmentation distribution and lacks a discernible internal structural pattern such as a pigment network, globules, or regression structures. The overall appearance is consistent with a benign pigmented lesion, but the irregular borders and heterogeneous pigmentation warrant careful clinical correlation and possible dermoscopic evaluation to rule out atypical nevi or other pigmented lesions requiring further investigation.
