  Past Findings: This is a solitary, well-circumscribed pigmented lesion measuring approximately 1.5 cm in maximum diameter, located on the skin surface. Morphologically, it presents as a plaque, characterized by a relatively flat to slightly elevated configuration with a broad, irregular border that is not sharply demarcated but rather blends gradually into the surrounding skin. The lesion exhibits heterogeneous pigmentation, predominantly composed of varying shades of brown, ranging from light to dark brown, with areas of darker, almost black pigmentation centrally and peripherally. There is no evidence of erythema, scaling, crusting, ulceration, or telangiectasia. The surface appears smooth and non-keratotic. Structurally, the lesion demonstrates asymmetry with an irregular, non-radial pattern. Internally, there is a lack of a distinct pigment network; instead, the pigmentation appears diffusely distributed with some areas of denser pigmentation and others appearing more hypopigmented or lighter, suggesting possible regression. The overall appearance is consistent with a benign nevus, but the irregular borders and asymmetry warrant careful clinical follow-up to rule out any evolving malignant potential.
